# prompts/conformance/test_churn_python.prompt
% You are an expert Python engineer. Your goal is to write the test-churn gate
% for the PDD CLI in `pdd/conformance/test_churn.py`.

% Role & Scope
  When regeneration is about to overwrite an existing, non-empty test file, this
  gate measures how much of it would be rewritten and refuses the write when that
  exceeds a configurable threshold. It exists because a model asked to add one
  test will happily rewrite the whole file, discarding years of accumulated
  regression coverage.

<include>context/python_preamble.prompt</include>

% Requirements

  1. **`_is_test_output_path(output_path) -> bool`** - decide whether a path is a
     test artifact. Falsy input returns `False`. Return `True` when ANY of:
     - Any path PART is `tests`, `__tests__`, or the SINGULAR `__test__` (the
       jest/vitest/Next.js co-located dir, #1903).
     - The filename starts with `test_`.
     - The LOWERCASED filename ends with a JS/TS suffix - `.test.` or `.spec.`
       followed by `ts`, `tsx`, `js`, `jsx`, `mjs` or `cjs`. The ESM/CJS variants
       were added for adopted co-located tests (#1903). This family is
       case-INSENSITIVE.
     - The LOWERCASED filename ends with `_test<ext>` or `_spec<ext>` for any
       `ext` in `_LANGUAGE_TEST_FILE_EXTS` (imported from `errors`). This covers
       sibling-file conventions like Go's `handler_test.go`, Ruby's
       `widget_spec.rb`, Rust, Elixir, Dart, Clojure and Lua. Driven by the shared
       tuple so adding a language to `language_format.csv` needs no change here.
     - The filename ends, **case-SENSITIVELY**, with one of the PascalCase
       JVM/.NET/Swift suffixes: `Test.java`, `Tests.java`, `TestCase.java`,
       `IT.java` (Maven failsafe), `Test.kt`, `Tests.kt`, `Spec.kt`,
       `Test.scala`, `Tests.scala`, `Spec.scala`, `Test.groovy`, `Tests.groovy`,
       `Spec.groovy`, `Tests.swift`, `Test.cs`, `Tests.cs`.
       Case sensitivity is REQUIRED here: lowercasing would false-positive on
       `latest.kt`, `manifest.java`, `request.scala`, `latest.groovy`. Languages
       with lowercase test filenames are already covered by the branch above.

  2. **`_prompt_allows_test_churn(prompt_content) -> bool`** - the opt-out. Scan
     anchored directives via `_iter_breaking_change_directives` (from
     `directives`), so buried mid-line marker mentions never disable the gate.
     A directive opts out only when an opt-out VERB directly governs `test`/`tests`
     as its object:
     - Opt-out verbs, case-insensitive, imperative and gerund alike:
       `rewrite`/`rewrites`/`rewrote`.../`rewriting`, `replace`.../`replacing`,
       `regenerate`.../`regenerating`, `overwrite`/`overwrites`/`overwriting`/
       `overwritten`, `churn`, `remove`.../`removing`, `drop`/`drops`/`dropped`/
       `dropping`.
     - For EVERY verb match in the directive, take the text after it, find the
       first `tests?` word, and inspect the text BETWEEN them. If that bridge
       contains a `,` or `;` or any of the conjunctions `and`, `but`, `then`,
       `or`, `plus`, `also`, the object belongs to a DIFFERENT verb - skip this
       verb match and try the next. Otherwise the directive opts out.
     - The resulting behavior, which MUST hold:
       | Directive | Opts out? |
       |---|---|
       | `BREAKING-CHANGE: rewriting the failing tests` | yes |
       | `BREAKING-CHANGE: rewrite the test suite for new helper` | yes |
       | `BREAKING-CHANGE: drop foo and rewrite tests` | yes - the SECOND verb governs `tests` |
       | `BREAKING-CHANGE: rewrite docs and update tests` | NO - `rewrite`'s object is `docs`, and `update` is not an opt-out verb |

  3. **`_get_test_churn_threshold() -> float`** - read
     `PDD_TEST_CHURN_THRESHOLD`, default `"0.40"`.
     - Accept a decimal ratio (`"0.40"`) or a percent string (`"40%"`, `"100%"`):
       strip a trailing `%`, parse, then divide by 100.
     - An empty/whitespace value returns the default `0.40`.
     - An unparseable value logs a `logger.warning` naming the raw value and
       returns `0.40`. It must NOT silently produce `0.0`, which would disable
       the gate.
     - Clamp the result into `[0.0, 1.0]`.

  4. **`_compute_test_churn_ratio(pre_text, post_text) -> float`** - stdlib only.
     - Split both sides with `splitlines()`; run `difflib.unified_diff` with
       `lineterm=""`.
     - Count lines beginning with `+` as added and `-` as removed, SKIPPING the
       `+++`, `---` and `@@` header lines.
     - If nothing was removed, return `0.0` - pure additive growth is never churn.
     - Otherwise return `min(max(added, removed) / max(len(before_lines), 1), 1.0)`.
     - An empty pre-text therefore yields `0.0`; first generation is exempt.
     - `_calculate_test_churn_ratio(before, after)` is a backward-compatible
       wrapper that delegates to it.

  5. **`_verify_test_churn(...) -> None`** - the gate itself. Return early,
     permitting the write, when ANY of: `existing_code` is missing or
     whitespace-only; `PDD_SKIP_TEST_CHURN_GATE` is enabled;
     `PDD_SKIP_CONFORMANCE` is enabled; the output path is not a test path; or the
     prompt opts out. Otherwise compute the threshold and ratio and, when
     `ratio > threshold`, raise `TestChurnError` with `prompt_name`,
     `output_path` (or `""`), `churn_ratio`, `threshold`,
     `pre_line_count`/`post_line_count` as the two `splitlines()` lengths, and
     the caller-supplied `adopted_human`.
     - `PDD_SKIP_TEST_CHURN_GATE=1` disables ONLY this gate;
       `PDD_SKIP_CONFORMANCE=1` disables all conformance gates.
     - `adopted_human` (#1903 §B.4) only ANNOTATES the raised error - it never
       changes the gate decision here. Default `False` keeps the strict hard-fail.

  6. **`_is_python_generation(language, output_path) -> bool`** - `True` when the
     lowercased language is `python` or `py`, or the output path ends `.py`.

  7. **`_find_default_test_files(tests_dir, code_file_path) -> List[str]`** -
     locate existing test files for a module by globbing the tests directory for
     the `test_<stem>.py` convention; return an empty list when either argument is
     missing or the directory does not exist.

% Dependencies
  - Imports `difflib`, `logging`, `os`, `pathlib`.
  - Imports `List`, `Optional` from `typing`.
  - From `.errors`: `TestChurnError`, `_LANGUAGE_TEST_FILE_EXTS`.
  - From `.directives`: `_env_flag_enabled`, `_iter_breaking_change_directives`.

% Instructions
  - Compile the opt-out verb, target and bridge-breaking regexes once at module
    level.
  - The same churn policy is also enforced by the `cmd_test_main` write path and
    one-session agentic sync, which import `_verify_test_churn` from here.
    Append/merge mode and pure additive growth are exempt because they preserve
    the existing file body.

% Deliverables
  - Code: `pdd/conformance/test_churn.py`
